      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A p-value of 0.042 is statistically significant at the standard alpha level of 0.05, which means your results are unlikely to be due to random chance alone, but you should be careful not to overstate the magnitude of the effect. The 95% confidence interval [0.15, 0.85] tells us that we are 95% confident the true effect size lies within this range, and since the entire interval is above zero, it confirms the direction of the effect is positive. Together, these statistics suggest a small-to-moderate effect that is statistically reliable, though the wide interval indicates some uncertainty about the precise size of the impact.\n\nYou can frame this in your thesis by stating that the primary hypothesis was supported, noting the statistical significance while acknowledging the variability in the effect size estimate. For example, \"The analysis revealed a statistically significant positive effect (p = 0.042), with the 95% confidence interval [0.15, 0.85] indicating that the true effect size is likely between 0.15 and 0.85.\" This approach balances the precision of the p-value with the range of",
